1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,283
Good morning everyone. Now whether you're going to university to study business or some other subject,

2
00:00:06,283 --> 00:00:11,780
many of you will eventually end up working for a company of some kind.

3
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:19,860
Now, when you first start working somewhere you will realise that the organisation you've joined has certain characteristics.

4
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:28,965
And we often refer to these social characteristics as the culture of the organisation. This includes its unwritten ideas,

5
00:00:28,965 --> 00:00:32,000
beliefs, values and things like that.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,640
One well known writer has classified company cultures by identifying four major types.

7
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:43,040
The first type is called the Power Culture

8
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:46,640
and it's usually found in small organisations.

9
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:51,978
It's the type of culture that needs a central source of power to be effective,

10
00:00:51,978 --> 00:00:58,740
and because control is in the hands of just one or two people there aren't many rules or procedures.

11
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:05,863
Another characteristic is that communication usually takes the form of conversations rather than,

12
00:01:05,863 --> 00:01:09,150
say, formal meetings or written memos.

13
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:15,573
Now one of the benefits of this culture is that the organisation has the ability to act quickly,

14
00:01:15,573 --> 00:01:17,960
so it responds well to threat

15
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:21,640
or danger on the one hand, and opportunity on the other.

16
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:26,538
But on the negative side, this type of organisation doesn't always act effectively,

17
00:01:26,538 --> 00:01:31,435
because it depends too much on one or two people at the top,

18
00:01:31,435 --> 00:01:36,710
and when these people make poor decisions there's no-one else who can influence them.

19
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:41,840
And the kind of person who does well in this type of business culture is one

20
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:44,013
who is happy to take risks,

21
00:01:44,013 --> 00:01:47,000
and for whom job security is a low priority.

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,400
The next type is known as Role Culture. That's R-O-L-E, not R-O-double L,

23
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:59,240
by the way, and this type is usually found in large companies

24
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:01,400
which have lots of different levels in them.

25
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:07,527
These organisations usually have separate departments that specialise in things like finance,

26
00:02:07,527 --> 00:02:10,590
or sales, or maintenance, or whatever.

27
00:02:10,590 --> 00:02:16,693
Each one is co-ordinated at the top by a small group of senior managers,

28
00:02:16,693 --> 00:02:21,923
and typically everyone's job is controlled by sets of rules and procedures -

29
00:02:21,923 --> 00:02:27,590
for example, there are specific job descriptions, rules for discipline, and so on.

30
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:30,750
What are the benefits of this kind of culture?

31
00:02:30,750 --> 00:02:34,081
Well firstly, because it's found in large organisations,

32
00:02:34,081 --> 00:02:39,060
its fixed costs or overhead, as they are known

33
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:43,240
are low in relation to its outputs, or what it produces.

34
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:47,400
In other words it can achieve economies of scale.

35
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:52,280
And secondly, it is particularly successful in business markets

36
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:54,630
where technical expertise is important.

37
00:02:54,630 --> 00:03:03,290
On the other hand, this culture is often very slow to recognise the need forchange, and even slower to react.

38
00:03:03,290 --> 00:03:06,620
What kind of person does this type of culture suit?

39
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:10,820
Well it suits employees who value security

40
00:03:10,820 --> 00:03:13,900
and who don't particularly want to have responsibility.

41
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:17,380
Moving on now to Task Cultures

42
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:21,500
This type is found in organistations that are project-oriented.

43
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:26,740
You usually find it where the market for the company's product is extremely competitive

44
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:30,220
or where the products themselves have a short life-span.

45
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:36,170
Usually top management delegates the projects, the people and other resources.

46
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:38,676
And once these have been allocated,

47
00:03:38,676 --> 00:03:42,120
little day-to-day control is exercised from the top

48
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:45,360
because this would seem like 'breaking the rules'.

49
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:49,910
Now one of the major benefits of this culture is that it's flexible.

50
00:03:49,910 --> 00:03:53,290
But it does have some major disadvantages too.

51
00:03:53,290 --> 00:03:59,500
For instance, it can't produce economies of scale or great depth of expertise.

52
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:04,750
People who like working in groups or teams prefer this type of culture.

53
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:10,000
And finally, the fourth category is called the Person Culture...
